# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 has "confined"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 have "defends → ¬values_benefit" and "confined"; they differ when only "people defend this mode of treating benefactors", "those who defend this treatment set much value on the benefit" and "this view is mostly confined to persons who think new truths may have been desirable once but that we have had enough of them now" are true, which the first allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "people defend this mode of treating benefactors", "those who defend this treatment set much value on the benefit" and "this view is mostly confined to persons who think new truths may have been desirable once but that we have had enough of them now" are true. Is S5, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now.", true then?
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 has "theory ∧ defends" and "great_service ∧ martyrdom_justified → ¬generous" and "defends ∧ great_service → ¬values_benefit"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 have "great_service ∧ martyrdom_justified → ¬generous"; they differ when only "truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm", "to discover to the world something which deeply concerns it, or prove it mistaken on some vital point, is as important a service as a human being can render to his fellow-creatures", "that the authors of such splendid benefits should be requited by martyrdom is the normal and justifiable state of things", "people defend this mode of treating benefactors" and "those who defend this treatment set much value on the benefit" are true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 has "¬values_benefit"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 have "theory → ¬generous"; they differ when only "truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm" and "we can commend the generosity of the theory's dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for new truths" are true, which the first allows
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 put it at S5, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now."; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 at S1, "A theory which maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm, cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths; but we cannot commend the generosity of its dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for them."
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S5, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now.", rather than S1, "A theory which maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm, cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths; but we cannot commend the generosity of its dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for them."?
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 takes for granted "truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm and people defend this mode of treating benefactors"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 do not
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm and people defend this mode of treating benefactors"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
